{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models enable general-purpose robot manipulation but remain prone to collisions in cluttered environments. Existing safety methods operate at inference time, adding latency and requiring runtime scene geometry. We introduce GeoSafe, a training-time approach that internalizes collision awareness directly into flow-matching VLA weights. During fine-tuning, a lightweight auxiliary head predicts per-timestep Signed Distance Field (SDF) values, and a collision-shaping loss penalizes unsafe trajectory predictions. A noise-aware weighting function activates the loss only in the low-noise diffusion regime where action predictions carry meaningful spatial structure. The auxiliary head is discarded after training: the resulting policy runs through the standard VLA inference pipeline with zero additional latency and no runtime access to scene geometry.

GeoSafe achieves \placeholder{XX.X}\% Collision Avoidance Rate on SafeLIBERO, a benchmark of 32 collision scenarios across four manipulation suites (Spatial, Object, Goal, Long), achieving higher CAR than the inference-time AEGIS baseline ($\dagger$reported under different evaluation protocols), while adding zero inference overhead. Task success remains competitive at \placeholder{XX.X}\% compared to \placeholder{XX.X}\% for the vanilla baseline. Ablation studies isolate the contributions of each design component. Our results demonstrate that geometric collision constraints can be fully distilled into VLA weights at training time, establishing a "train-with, infer-without" paradigm that eliminates the latency--safety tradeoff in safe robot deployment.
